# Copy this file to .env. The CLI loads a working directory .env at startup, so you do not
# need to source it. A value already exported in the shell wins over the file. Every setting
# line is an optional override. Defaults are shown where they exist. Values without defaults
# use `...` as the placeholder. Uncomment only what you actually change.
# The default provider order is OpenAI first, then Anthropic. With OPENAI_API_KEY or
# CYBERJURY_API_KEY set, the default is OpenAI. Without an OpenAI key, the default is
# Anthropic. Every review path uses provider APIs and requires a reachable key.

# Provider keys are read by each SDK. Set the key for your provider to call its API.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is used with --provider anthropic.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
# OPENAI_API_KEY is used with --provider openai, and makes OpenAI the default.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=...

# The primary model backend defaults to OpenAI `gpt-5.6` when an OpenAI key is reachable,
# otherwise Anthropic `claude-opus-5`.
# Valid providers are openai and anthropic.
# CYBERJURY_PROVIDER=openai
# CYBERJURY_MODEL=gpt-5.6
# CYBERJURY_API_KEY overrides the SDK key above.
# CYBERJURY_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_API_BASE sets a gateway or proxy endpoint for the selected provider.
# CYBERJURY_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_WIRE_API optionally forces chat or responses. Leave it unset so the client
# selects Responses for reasoning model names and Chat Completions for other model names.
# CYBERJURY_WIRE_API=...

# Model roles work in both review paths. The finder finds, the challenger refutes, and the
# judge confirms before a deletion. A single model run sets none of these. Override only
# the seat you want to change.
#
# Each role takes the same five fields. Provider inherits when unset. Model, API key,
# base, and wire API inherit only when the role uses the same provider as the base.
# A role that switches provider uses that provider's default model unless a role model is set.
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_PROVIDER=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_MODEL=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_WIRE_API=...
#
# This example keeps OpenAI as the base finder, uses Claude as the challenger, and sets a
# distinct OpenAI judge. The challenger uses a different vendor than base, so it needs its
# own key. The judge model must differ from the finder to confirm deletion independently.
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_PROVIDER=anthropic
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_WIRE_API=...
# CYBERJURY_JUDGE_PROVIDER=openai
# CYBERJURY_JUDGE_MODEL=...
# CYBERJURY_JUDGE_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_JUDGE_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_JUDGE_WIRE_API=...
# Without a distinct judge, no challenger refutation is accepted, the recall-safe default.
# `review repository --run` finds with one model, the finder.

# Review strategy is intentionally per command, not env-backed: pass --mode, --rounds,
# and --concurrency on the command that should use them.

# Request behavior uses these optional overrides with defaults shown.
# CYBERJURY_RETRIES is the retry count after a failed model call.
# CYBERJURY_RETRIES=2
# CYBERJURY_TIMEOUT is the per call deadline in seconds and the retry hard cap.
# CYBERJURY_TIMEOUT=240

# The evm domain uses this optional source fetch key when source is not already on disk.
# One key covers every chain, or pass --api-key.
# CYBERJURY_ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=...
